    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-69209-2",
    "abstract": "Europe has experienced abnormal warming over the recent decades. Model-based studies highlight that multi-centennial internal variability of the North Atlantic can strongly affect European temperatures. However, the limited availability of high-resolution proxy records has hindered observational assessment of the existence and amplitude of such variability in the real climate system. Here, we compile annual-to-decadal proxy-based Holocene reconstructions, instrumental observations, and climate model simulations to demonstrate the existence of this multi-centennial variability mode and quantify its amplitude. We show that this mode is closely tied to the internal variability of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation. Its temporal evolution explains part of the observed 20<sup>th</sup> century variability, and a shift towards a positive phase in the late 1990s can explain the recent amplified warming over Europe. When its amplitude is constrained by observations, this internal variability may enhance anthropogenic warming in Northern Europe by up to 30% over 2000-2035.",
    "field": "Palaeoclimatology",
    "problem": "The limited availability of high-resolution proxy records has hindered observational assessment of multi-centennial internal variability, which could explain why climate models tend to underestimate the observed accelerated warming over Europe.",
    "method": "We compile palaeoclimate reconstructions, instrumental observations, and climate model simulations to identify a multi-centennial internal variability mode and quantify its contribution to the recently observed amplified warming over Europe.",
    "task": "The task is to identify a multi-centennial signal of natural climate variability using palaeoclimate data and models, and to determine its origin and potential impact on recent and near-future temperature trends in Europe.",
